  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 AM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nCurrently strong upper high is located over the far SE US with an\nupper trough over the West Coast. This has the upper flow over the\narea S to SW and as we go into Fri/Sat, the low level flow will be\nturning SE and bringing in some Gulf moisture. This will be the\nbest monsoonal setup we have had this summer with PW's on the rise\nto 1.1-1.3\" across the lowlands.  \n\nFor this evening and overnight, it looks very similar to yesterday\nwith isld to sct storms from the NM Bootheel to Sierra County with\njust isolated storms south and east. There does look to be another\nwave that will move up out of Mexico overnight that could bring\nsome showers and thunderstorms to parts of far west TX after\nmidnight. Overnight lows again in the upper 60s to lower 70s with\n50s/60s in the mountains. For Friday, we actually get a bit of a\nlee trough developing over eastern zones and this will be the\nfocus for storm initiation in the afternoon with outflows pushing\nstorms further west toward the RGV. Highs will remain under 100\ndegrees with the increased moisture in place.\n\nSaturday is looking like the most active day, low level ESE flow\ngets established and moisture peaks. There isn't much of an upper\ndisturbance, but the deep moisture and SE flow over the Sacs will\nfire early afternoon storms and then good agreement with models\nthat outflows will move W to SW and be the focus for additional\ndevelopment over the lowlands into the overnight hours.\nInstability isn't great with the deeper moisture but slow storm\nmotion will bring some locally heavy rain and gusty winds will\nalso be a threat.\n\nSome differences start showing up in the models going into Sun and\nearly next week. They do agree the upper high retrogrades and\nbecomes established back over the southern Rockies but the GFS\npushes the moisture west into AZ with some storms on the higher\nterrain, but the EC keeps some moisture under the high and allows\nfor at least isolated lowland coverage daily. Also, going into\nmidweek, the GFS breaks down the ridge with a fairly potent trough\nmoving through the Northern Rockies while the EC keeps the high in\nplace. Looking at ensemble means, even the GEFS mean supports the\nridge remaining in place and given it's still mid Aug, think the\nbreakdown of such a strong ridge is unlikely. This basically keeps\nmost of the area on the drier side with temperatures above normal\nwith highs back into the 100-105 range for the lowlands.",
